          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"stael-corinne-b2623292ecb28893\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Oswald presents his love as an evident, enduring emotional fact—“vous voyez que je vous aime”—in support of his demand that Corinne reveal her identity. The surrounding readiness to offer his hand and life gives the avowal some commitment-related resonance, but the highlighted utterance primarily asserts rather than itself undertakes. Suddenness and intense emotion do not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"P is weakly supported because the avowal is coordinated with Oswald’s stated readiness to offer marriage and his life, though it does not clearly enact that offer by itself.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Oswald directly addresses Corinne in represented dialogue; the novel’s quotation marks frame a direct character utterance rather than a report of one.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Oswald makes the avowal during an emotionally charged dispute about Corinne’s concealed identity. It functions argumentatively: since she can see that he loves her and is prepared to offer his hand and life, he treats her refusal to disclose herself as cruel. His prior private resolve to marry and protect her strengthens the sincerity and commitment context, while his later distress reveals conflict between this love and paternal or national obligations. None of the suddenness, repetition, or bodily intensity shows that the love words themselves are a reflex-like discharge rather than an impassioned avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“vous voyez que je vous aime” (“you see that I love you”)\",\"supports\":\"The evidential construction explicitly presents Oswald’s loving state as something Corinne can recognize, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“vous voyez que vingt fois par jour je suis prêt à vous offrir et ma main et ma vie”\",\"supports\":\"The immediately coordinated claim of readiness to offer marriage and life supplies limited P support, while remaining grammatically a statement of readiness rather than an enacted promise or offer.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Before visiting Corinne, Oswald exclaims privately: “Il faut que j’épouse Corinne; il faut que je sois son protecteur.”\",\"supports\":\"His prior resolve to marry and protect Corinne corroborates the avowal’s sincere commitment-oriented context without converting the highlighted clause itself into a full undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration says he speaks “tout à coup” and describes a “touchante expression de sensibilité,” later adding cold sweat, pallor, and trembling lips.\",\"supports\":\"These details establish emotional intensity and delivery but provide no independent evidence that the love words function as expressive/reflexive discharge; therefore they do not warrant E.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal, with modest P resonance from its immediate commitment context. Intensity and sudden delivery can be represented contextually without E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
